The following section is structured for direct incorporation into a memorandum of points and authorities. It applies the underlying legal principles without unnecessarily repeating statutory references, allowing counsel to select the authorities appropriate to the particular motion.

## COUNSEL'S VOLUNTARY ELECTION TO DISCONTINUE REPRESENTATION DID NOT CONFER AUTHORITY TO EXTINGUISH THE PLAINTIFFS' SUBSTANTIVE RIGHTS

### A. Original purpose and scope of representation

The underlying action was commenced on September 21, 2020, for the adjudication of injuries and damages sustained by two separately identified plaintiffs. The original pleading expressly distinguished Laura Alcaraz, appearing in her individual capacity, from Laura Elisa Rodriguez, appearing by and through her guardian ad litem.

The commencement of the action established the objective for which legal representation was undertaken. That objective was the prosecution and protection of the plaintiffs' respective claims, subject to their lawful instructions and any judicial safeguards applicable to their representative circumstances.

The authority associated with such representation did not constitute an unrestricted delegation of the plaintiffs' substantive rights, nor did it transfer ownership or ultimate control over the disposition of those rights to counsel.

### B. Counsel's affirmative election to discontinue representation

On April 22, 2024, Harry Nalbandyan, identifying himself as counsel of record, filed separate motions seeking to be relieved as counsel for Laura Alcaraz and Laura Rodriguez.

The motions were initiated by counsel through applications for judicial withdrawal rather than through a consensual substitution of attorneys.

In the accompanying declarations, counsel represented under penalty of perjury that irreconcilable differences had arisen concerning the further handling of the action.

These representations were made separately concerning each plaintiff and were submitted while the underlying action remained pending, with a Final Status Conference scheduled for May 17, 2024, and trial scheduled for May 31, 2024.

The withdrawal hearings were scheduled for May 29, 2024, two days before the anticipated commencement of trial.

The resulting record establishes that counsel had affirmatively sought to discontinue representation and had formally represented to the Court that substantial disagreement existed concerning the further handling of the litigation.

### C. Distinction between withdrawal and authority to dispose of substantive rights

Counsel's election to seek withdrawal did not itself extinguish the underlying claims, resolve the matters in controversy, establish the plaintiffs' consent to settlement, or authorize the surrender of their substantive rights.

Although counsel's procedural status may have continued pending judicial determination of the withdrawal motions, that status must be distinguished from the existence of actual authority to compromise the litigation.

The continuation of an attorney's appearance as counsel of record does not independently establish authorization to undertake every action capable of affecting the client's interests.

Likewise, disagreement concerning the further handling of an action cannot be treated as affirmative consent to its termination.

Any subsequent exercise of dispositive authority must therefore be evaluated according to the authorization actually existing at the time of the particular act, rather than inferred exclusively from counsel's continued appearance in the proceedings.

### D. Separate consideration of the plaintiffs' rights and interests

The original complaint and the separately filed withdrawal motions establish that the litigation concerned two distinct plaintiffs.

The existence of a common attorney, common defendant, common underlying occurrence, or common procedural history did not eliminate the separate nature of their respective rights and interests.

Any subsequent compromise affecting both plaintiffs required consideration of the authority, consent, and legal capacity applicable to each.

An instrument attributed to one plaintiff cannot, without further foundation, establish the consent of another. Similarly, an act undertaken in an individual capacity cannot automatically be attributed to the exercise of representative authority.

Where judicial protection of a represented party was required, the existence of counsel or a guardian ad litem did not independently eliminate that protection.

The validity of any subsequent disposition must therefore be examined separately as to each plaintiff, each affected interest, and each capacity in which authorization was purportedly exercised.

### E. Contemporaneous notice to opposing counsel

The April 22, 2024 electronic proofs of service identify Gabriel Wainfeld as the attorney served on behalf of the Los Angeles County Metropolitan Transportation Authority.

The documents identified for service included the withdrawal motions, supporting declarations, and proposed orders. A separate electronic proof of service expressly identified the documents concerning Laura Rodriguez.

The significance of this evidence is not dependent upon an immediate determination of misconduct or participation by opposing counsel.

Rather, it establishes a documented representation of service relevant to determining what information was communicated, available, received, or subsequently relied upon concerning the status and circumstances of plaintiffs' representation.

Any later reliance upon counsel's asserted authority should be considered in conjunction with the information previously communicated and any additional representations made before the subsequent disposition of the action.

### F. Intervening events requiring independent examination

The April 22 filings establish a contemporaneous point of reference from which subsequent events may be evaluated.

Where counsel thereafter represented that the controversy had been resolved, the factual and evidentiary basis for that representation becomes material to the determination of whether an intervening agreement was actually reached.

Relevant considerations include the identity of the participating persons, the capacity in which each acted, the terms communicated, the instructions received, the existence or absence of mutual assent, any contemporaneous objection or rejection, and the authority supporting subsequent implementation.

The existence of a written instrument must be distinguished from the circumstances under which it was prepared, executed, retained, transmitted, accepted, and subsequently relied upon.

A signature, standing alone, does not necessarily resolve every disputed issue concerning the formation, scope, effectiveness, representative authority, or implementation of the instrument.

Each consequential act must be examined according to the facts and authority existing when that act occurred.

### G. Reconciliation of subsequent representations with the contemporaneous record

The Court's attention is directed to the relationship between the representations made on April 22, 2024, and any subsequent representations asserting that the underlying controversy had been resolved.

The earlier declarations are not offered as conclusive proof that a subsequent agreement was impossible. They are offered as contemporaneous evidence establishing circumstances that preceded the asserted disposition and that warrant consideration together with the intervening evidence.

The existence of irreconcilable differences does not preclude the possibility of a later lawful agreement. However, neither does a later representation of settlement independently establish that such differences were resolved through informed and authorized consent.

Where the intervening authorization is disputed, the determination should rest upon competent evidence concerning the events themselves rather than upon assumptions derived exclusively from counsel's procedural status or subsequent filings.

### H. Preservation of the parties' substantive and procedural rights

The requested examination is directed toward determining whether the subsequent disposition accurately reflected the plaintiffs' lawful decisions and whether the procedural mechanisms employed were supported by the necessary underlying authority.

The plaintiffs' substantive rights should not be treated as having been surrendered merely because procedural documents were prepared, transmitted, filed, accepted, or entered.

The existence of a procedural record and the validity of the acts upon which that record depends are distinct matters.

Accordingly, where the evidence establishes that a subsequent disposition exceeded the authority actually conferred, appropriate relief should be considered to restore the affected parties to the position permitted by law, subject to the Court's jurisdiction and the applicable grounds for relief.

### I. Purpose of presenting the April 22, 2024 record

The April 22, 2024 filings are presented to establish the documented circumstances preceding the subsequent disposition of the action.

The requested determination is not founded upon speculation concerning counsel's motives, nor upon an assumption that the election to withdraw independently establishes wrongdoing.

It is founded upon the need to reconcile the contemporaneous documentary record with the authority asserted and exercised in connection with later events.

The ultimate question is whether the disposition of the plaintiffs' substantive rights resulted from their lawful and authorized decisions, or whether those rights were affected through actions undertaken without sufficient underlying authority.

That determination should be made upon the complete evidentiary record, separately accounting for each plaintiff, each relevant capacity, and each consequential act.

